---
import GuideLayout from '../../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import type { FaqItem, HowToData } from '../../../types';

const faq: FaqItem[] = [
  {
    question: 'How do you place a flag in Google Minesweeper?',
    answer:
      'On a desktop computer with a mouse, right-click any unopened square to place or remove a flag. On a laptop trackpad, tap with two fingers (or hold Control + click on Mac / Alt + click on Chromebook). On a phone or tablet, long-press the square or tap the 🚩 flag toggle icon at the bottom.',
  },
  {
    question:
      'How do you flag on Google Minesweeper without a mouse (Chromebook & MacBook)?',
    answer:
      'Use a two-finger tap on any laptop trackpad. Alternatively, hold Control + click on a MacBook or hold Alt + click on a Chromebook. Note that Google Search Minesweeper does not have a Spacebar shortcut — pressing Space scrolls the page instead.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I open and play Minesweeper on Google?',
    answer:
      'Type "minesweeper" or "google minesweeper" into Google Search and click the blue "Play" button at the top of the results. No download, extension, or sign-in is required.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you play Google Minesweeper offline?',
    answer:
      'Google’s search easter egg requires an internet connection to load the search results page initially. Once the board is loaded in an open browser tab — or on a client-side web version like Meathill Minesweeper — you can finish your current game even if your Wi-Fi drops.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Google Minesweeper different from classic Minesweeper?',
    answer:
      'The core rules are identical, though Google uses 10×8 (Easy), 18×14 (Medium), and 24×20 (Hard) grids instead of classic 9×9, 16×16, and 16×30 boards, and lacks keyboard hotkeys, probability hints, and post-game replays.',
  },
];

const howTo: HowToData = {
  name: 'How to play Minesweeper on Google and place flags',
  steps: [
    'Search "minesweeper" on Google and click the blue Play button above the search results.',
    'Choose Easy, Medium, or Hard from the top-left dropdown and click any square — your first click is always safe.',
    'Read each revealed number to count how many mines sit in the 8 surrounding squares.',
    'Right-click, two-finger tap on a trackpad, or long-press on mobile to flag mines, then clear every safe square to win.',
  ],
};
---

<GuideLayout
  lang="en"
  title="How to Play Minesweeper on Google: Place Flags & No-Mouse Guide (2026)"
  heading="How to Play Minesweeper on Google (Flags & No-Mouse Controls)"
  description="Learn how to play Google Minesweeper (2026): open it in Search, how to place a flag on PC, MacBook trackpad, Chromebook & mobile without a mouse, plus pro tips."
  path="/guide/google-minesweeper/"
  keywords="google minesweeper, how to play minesweeper on google, how to place a flag in google minesweeper, how to flag on minesweeper google without a mouse, how to play minesweeper without a mouse"
  updated="2026-10-30"
  lede="Want to jump straight into a round? Click any cell on the 9×9 board right below to start immediately (with a 1-click 🚩 Flag toggle built in), or read on for every Google Minesweeper control, flagging shortcut, and trackpad trick."
  faq={faq}
  howTo={howTo}
>
  <p>
    <strong>Google Minesweeper</strong> is the free classic logic puzzle built directly into Google Search: type “minesweeper” into the search bar and click Play to open an instant browser board across three difficulty levels (Easy, Medium, and Hard) on desktop, laptop, or mobile with no download required.
  </p>

  <h2>How to Open and Play Minesweeper on Google</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Go to Google Search and type <strong>“minesweeper”</strong> or <strong>“google minesweeper”</strong>.</li>
    <li>Click the blue <strong>Play</strong> button inside the interactive Doodle card at the top of the results.</li>
    <li>Select <strong>Easy</strong> (10×8, 10 mines), <strong>Medium</strong> (18×14, 40 mines), or <strong>Hard</strong> (24×20, 99 mines) and click any green tile — <strong>your first click is guaranteed mine-free</strong>.</li>
    <li>Use the numbers (each counts mines in the 8 adjacent squares) to flag every mine and uncover all safe tiles.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How to Place a Flag in Google Minesweeper</h2>
  <p>
    Flagging marks squares you know contain mines so you never click them by mistake — and lets you clear surrounding safe squares much faster. In Google Minesweeper, <strong>right-clicking</strong> an unopened square on desktop (or <strong>long-pressing</strong> on a touchscreen) drops a red flag 🚩; repeating the gesture removes it.
  </p>

  <h3>How to Flag in Minesweeper Without a Mouse (Laptop Trackpad &amp; Chromebook)</h3>
  <p>
    Many players try pressing the <code>Spacebar</code> or <code>F</code> key to flag mines, only to watch the Google Search page scroll down. <strong>Google’s built-in Minesweeper widget does not support keyboard shortcuts for flagging.</strong> Instead, use your device’s exact right-click / touch equivalent:
  </p>

  <div
    tabindex="0"
    role="region"
    aria-labelledby="en-controls-caption"
    class="guide-table-wrapper not-prose"
    style="--col-2: 'Dig / Open Square'; --col-3: 'Place / Remove Flag 🚩';"
  >
    <table>
      <caption id="en-controls-caption">Google Minesweeper Controls &amp; No-Mouse Flagging Cheat Sheet</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Device</th>
          <th>Dig / Open Square</th>
          <th>Place / Remove Flag 🚩</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Desktop Mouse (PC / Mac)</th>
          <td>Left-click</td>
          <td>Right-click an unopened square</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>MacBook Trackpad</th>
          <td>1-finger tap / click</td>
          <td><strong>Two-finger tap</strong> or hold <code>Control + Click</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Windows Laptop Touchpad</th>
          <td>1-finger tap / click</td>
          <td><strong>Two-finger tap</strong> or press bottom-right corner</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Chromebook Trackpad</th>
          <td>1-finger tap / click</td>
          <td><strong>Two-finger tap</strong> or hold <code>Alt + Click</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Phone &amp; Tablet (iOS / Android)</th>
          <td>Single tap</td>
          <td><strong>Long-press</strong> square or tap bottom 🚩 / ⛏️ toggle</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <figure>
    <img
      src="/guide-flag-controls.svg"
      alt="how to place a flag in google minesweeper without a mouse"
      width="760"
      height="260"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Quick visual reference: how to flag mines in Google Minesweeper on MacBook, Windows laptops, Chromebooks, and phones without an external mouse.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Trackpad speed tip</strong>: Enable <em>Tap to click</em> in your system settings so a light two-finger tap places a flag instantly without depressing the physical pad.</li>
    <li><strong>Single-button mouse or cramped laptop?</strong> Switch to <a href="/en/">Meathill Minesweeper</a> (or use the mini-board at the top of this page): once flags around a number match that number, <strong>double-clicking the number chords all remaining safe neighbors open</strong> — zero right-click chord gymnastics required.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Core Rules &amp; Where Google Minesweeper Falls Short</h2>
  <p>
    Google’s version follows classic <a href="/en/guide/how-to-play/">Minesweeper rules and number patterns</a>, making it a great quick break. However, players trying to beat Hard consistently run into three roadblocks:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No probability hints</strong>: Endgame 50/50s stay pure guesswork, so you never learn whether another cell actually had a 15% vs. 65% mine risk.</li>
    <li><strong>No post-game replay</strong>: One misclick ends the run with no way to rewind to the turning point.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-standard grid sizes &amp; volatile stats</strong>: Google uses 10×8 / 18×14 / 24×20 instead of official 9×9 / 16×16 / 16×30 boards, and clearing browser data wipes your best times.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Want to turn guessing into calculating? Open <a href="/en/">Meathill Minesweeper</a> to play with <strong>Learn Mode</strong> (live mine-probability heatmap + 0–10 move efficiency scoring + full click-by-click replay), or test tricky board states in our <a href="/en/tools/probability-calculator/">Minesweeper Probability Calculator</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
